    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d261">

                <pb facs="0621" n="593" xml:id="pg593"/>

                <head>261. Telegram From the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of-Staff (Wheeler) to the Commander in
                    Chief, Pacific (Sharp)<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d261-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1964-08-02T18:49:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">August 2,
                                1964—6:49 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>7681. This is a DeSoto Patrol Message. Reference: JCS 7680 DTG 021725Z Aug 64<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d261-f2"/> In event US vessels are attacked in international
                    waters (11 miles offshore or more), you will seek to destroy the attacking
                    forces, however, pursuit into hostile waters or air space is not authorized.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d261-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d261-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Johnson Library, National Security File, Vietnam Country File, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Maddox</hi> Incident. Secret; Flash. Repeated to Saigon,
                        MACV, the White House, the Department of
                    State, CIA, and the Department of Defense, among
                    others.</p>
            </note>
